[interviewer · STRUCTURE · 0:47] Understood. You've said costs are where you'd start. Which single branch would you test first, and what data would you ask for to do it?

[candidate · 0:52] I'd test the operating cost structure as a whole first, and within that, the largest line — which for a coffee chain is usually either COGS or store labor, each plausibly 25–35% of revenue. A line that big only needs to move a few points to explain a chunk of an 18-point swing, whereas corporate overhead would need an implausibly large jump.

The data I'd ask for: the P&L for both years, with each cost line expressed as a percentage of revenue — COGS, store labor, rent and occupancy, marketing, corporate G&A, and D&A. Comparing the percentages across the two years isolates which line grew faster than revenue, so I'm not fooled by absolute dollars rising just because revenue rose 15%.

Once I see which percentage moved most, I'd go one level deeper into that line.

Do we have that P&L breakdown? And what would you like me to look at next?
