[interviewer · EXHIBIT · 1:04] A COGS-led decomposition. Coffee beans were 25% of COGS two years ago. Other input costs — dairy, packaging, and food — are up 37.5% over the past two years. Menu prices have not changed in two years. Given those, what does the evidence say about why COGS rose, and what would you test next?

[candidate · 1:12] Let me work it per revenue dollar. Two years ago COGS was 42% of revenue; beans were 25% of that, so 10.5 points of revenue, and everything else was 31.5 points.

Other inputs up 37.5%: 31.5 × 1.375 = 43.3 points of revenue today. Total COGS today is 58, so beans must be 58 − 43.3 = 14.7 points, up from 10.5 — that's a 40% increase.

So the story is input cost inflation across the board, roughly 37–40% on both beans and non-bean inputs, and crucially menu prices haven't moved in two years. They've absorbed every dollar of it. That's the root cause: a pricing failure as much as a cost shock, since a chain with pricing power normally passes some through.

What I'd test next, if you'd like: whether the 15% revenue growth is volume or new stores, and what competitors have done on price. What would you like me to look at next?
