[interviewer · EXHIBIT · 1:22] Right. You said margin goes negative in roughly eight months at this rate. Walk me through how you got that.

[candidate · 1:27] Sure. Profit margin is falling about nine points a year — 24 to 15 to 6. Current margin is 6 points. So 6 divided by 9 equals two-thirds of a year, which is about eight months until margin hits zero.

I should flag two caveats on that. First, it's a straight-line extrapolation of a two-point trend, so it assumes input costs keep rising at the same pace, which I wouldn't take as given — commodity cycles can flatten or reverse. Second, it's a margin figure, not cash. Net margin crossing zero isn't the same as running out of cash, since D&A is non-cash and the timing depends on their balance sheet and any debt service. So I'd treat eight months as a directional urgency marker rather than a hard deadline.

What would you like me to look at next?
