[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 1:29] Understood. The arithmetic holds, and you flagged the limits yourself. Beyond price and cost levers we've discussed, what else could Brew & Bean do to restore its margin? Give me as many distinct ideas as you can.

[candidate · 1:37] Let me group them so I don't just list randomly.

On price, beyond a straight increase: selective increases on low-elasticity items like espresso drinks, a size-and-price ladder, loyalty pricing so frequent customers feel less of it, and bundling food with coffee at a blended margin.

On input cost: renegotiate or consolidate bean suppliers for volume terms, forward-contract or hedge beans to stop the annual ratchet, dual-source dairy, cut packaging spec or push reusable cups, and reduce waste and spoilage — in coffee, milk waste and brewed-coffee dump rates can be material.

On mix: push higher-margin items — brewed drip, packaged beans, merchandise — and prune low-margin SKUs.

On overhead, which added two points: corporate headcount, marketing efficiency, and whether new-store support costs are being absorbed centrally.

On footprint: close or renegotiate underperforming leases, and reconsider the pace of new openings.

On throughput: labor is flat at 22%, so scheduling to peak hours or mobile ordering could lift sales per store without adding cost.

What would you like me to look at next?
