[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 1:39] Got it. Pull it together for the CEO. What do you recommend, in what order, and why?

[candidate · 1:46] My recommendation: raise menu prices, starting now and in a staged way, because the margin decline is a pricing failure more than a cost failure.

The evidence: of the 18-point margin decline, 16 points sit in COGS and two in overhead. Labor is flat at 22%. Input costs rose roughly 37 to 40% across both beans and non-bean inputs, and menu prices have not moved in two years. The company absorbed all of it. At nine points of margin a year, they're roughly eight months from zero.

Order of actions. First, immediate selective price increases on lower-elasticity drinks — this is the only lever that matches the size and speed of the problem. Second, in parallel, lock input costs through supplier renegotiation and forward contracts on beans so the ratchet stops. Third, mix shift and waste reduction for a few points. Fourth, trim the two points of overhead.

Risks: price elasticity is unknown, and competitors may have already raised prices, which would make this easier — or not, which makes volume loss real.

Next steps: elasticity test in a subset of the 200 stores, and a competitor price benchmark before a chain-wide rollout.
