[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 1:48] Understood. You named elasticity as a risk. If it turns out customers are far more price-sensitive than you assumed and volume drops, what happens to your recommendation, and how would you adapt it?

[candidate · 1:55] If elasticity is high, the price lever partly breaks, so I'd want to size it before abandoning it. At 58% COGS, contribution margin is about 42 cents on the dollar. A 10% price increase adds roughly 10 points of revenue at essentially no added cost, so it would take a fairly large volume loss to wipe that out — but it's not unlimited, and high sensitivity means I'd cap the increase rather than take it all at once.

How I'd adapt. First, go narrower: raise price only on the least elastic items and hold or even feature a visible entry-price drink so the chain doesn't look expensive. Second, lean harder on the levers that don't touch the customer — supplier renegotiation, hedging beans, waste reduction, mix shift toward packaged beans and drip. Third, use loyalty and bundling to raise effective ticket without a headline price change.

If even that falls short, the question becomes structural: close weak stores, slow new openings. That's slower and more painful, which is why I'd still test price first.

What would you like me to look at next?
